  "query_preview": "..\nUpper ridge over the northern plains will slowly drift eastward\nthrough the weekend as an upper trough digs into the western\nstates. Southwest flow and weak embedded shortwaves will bring\nperiodic chances for rain into the region. \n\nLight rain is expected to slowly move northeast into the region \nthis afternoon and linger overnight as it pushes east. Most \nlocations look to have a damp and chilly Halloween evening. Fog \nwill continue to impact portions of the region through the morning\nwith some locally dense fog, reducing visibility in spots to 2 \nmile or less.\n\nAs the Pacific upper trough moves through the Great Basin, a\nColorado low is expected to develop ahead of it. This will spread\nprecipitation northward, and will generally favor the Yellowstone\nbasin for Sunday night into Monday. Splitting in the upper trough\nnear the Canadian boarder will keep cold air from being drawn\nsouthward, and result in little to no mixed precipitation or snow. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nAdjusted PoPs in the first 24 hours of the forecast by blending\nfor slightly expanded coverage. Otherwise used NBM this cycle.\n\nA lot of uncertainty regarding fog, low cloud and precipitation\ntoday. \n\nSystem for Monday continues to slide southward, which is common,\nand could result in less precipitation, especially across the\nnorth, than expected.",
